detail.abstract:
Legal scholarship has established that corporations operate as Level 1 entities (optimization algorithms without theory of mind), yet they are composed entirely of human managers capable of Level 3 intentionality (recursive moral reasoning).&nbsp; 
<div>
 This paper examines the puzzle: how do moral agents produce amoral outputs? I identify four structural mechanisms that transform human intentionality within corporate environments: fiduciary duty as legal constraint, incentive structures as alignment mechanisms, diffusion of responsibility through organizational hierarchy, and the corporate veil as an intentionality shield.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Drawing on agency theory, moral psychology (Milgram, Bandura), and case studies (Wells Fargo, Boeing 737 MAX, Volkswagen), I argue that corporate structure functions as an "intentionality laundering" system. Traditional enforcement fails because it targets either the Level 1 entity (which cannot process moral sanctions) or individual Level 3 agents (whose intentionality has already been transformed by structure).&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 This analysis suggests that reforming corporate behavior requires redesigning the structures that transform moral capacity, not merely increasing penalties or improving ethics training.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 I propose five design principles for intentionality-preserving corporate structures, while acknowledging the fundamental trade-off between organizational efficiency and moral agency preservation.
</div>
